usbnet: allow capping rx_qlen, to unstarve the MUSB shared endpoint

On the F1C200s (MUSB host) wired CarPlay needs two bulk-IN endpoints of
the SAME device live at once: the iAP2 control channel over the usbfs
mux (ep5in) and the CarPlay AV session over cdc_ncm/usb0 (ep8in). The
controller will not service both -- ftrace on 6.18.42 (phone connected
directly, no hub) shows the mux completely starved while NCM is up:

  endpoint  role                 enq   rx
  ep5in     mux IN (iAP2)         27    0     <- zero completions
  ep8in     cdc_ncm bulk IN        5    5

The five ep8in URBs are exactly usbnet's rx_qlen for this device:
MAX_QUEUE_MEMORY / rx_urb_size = 91080 / 16384 = 5, with cdc_ncm setting
rx_max = 16384. Every one of them completed with len 0/16384 -- the NCM
link was carrying no traffic at all -- so this is not bandwidth
contention: five idle but permanently-posted URBs are enough to block
the other endpoint outright.

musb_host.c already names this failure mode ("Devices like Ethernet and
serial adapters keep reads posted at all times, which will starve other
devices without this logic") for its NAK-timeout rotation, which is
gated on qh->mux == 1 and so only applies to QHs that land on the shared
bulk_ep.

Add an rx_qlen_max module parameter (0 = unchanged, the default, so
stock behaviour is untouched) applied in usbnet_update_max_qlen(), which
is the single point where rx_qlen is (re)computed. Writable at 0644 so
it can be tuned at runtime via
/sys/module/usbnet/parameters/rx_qlen_max and picked up by re-binding
the device, or set on the kernel command line as usbnet.rx_qlen_max=N
(usbnet is built-in here).

This is primarily a diagnostic lever for confirming the starvation
hypothesis, but it is also a plausible mitigation on this board: 5 x
16 KB of NCM RX buffering is not defensible on 64 MiB of RAM.

--- a/drivers/net/usb/usbnet.c
+++ b/drivers/net/usb/usbnet.c
@@ -66,6 +66,16 @@
 module_param (msg_level, int, 0);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC (msg_level, "Override default message level");
 
+/*
+ * Cap on the number of RX URBs kept in flight. 0 keeps the computed
+ * value. Small values trade throughput for not monopolising a host
+ * controller that cannot service several bulk-IN endpoints at once
+ * (MUSB on suniv): perpetually-posted reads otherwise starve them.
+ */
+static int rx_qlen_max;
+module_param(rx_qlen_max, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_qlen_max, "Cap on in-flight RX URBs (0 = no cap)");
+
 /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
 
 static const char * const usbnet_event_names[] = {
@@ -386,6 +396,8 @@
 insanity:
 		dev->rx_qlen = dev->tx_qlen = 4;
 	}
+	if (rx_qlen_max > 0 && dev->rx_qlen > rx_qlen_max)
+		dev->rx_qlen = rx_qlen_max;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(usbnet_update_max_qlen);
 
